-- title:
--   Closed subfunctor criterion with finitely generated chart ideals
-- statement:
--   Let $H$ be a scheme and let $Q$ be a predicate on affine points of $H$, that is, an assignment to each commutative ring $A$ (in the ambient universe) and each morphism $u : \operatorname{Spec} A \to H$ of a proposition $Q\,A\,u$. Three hypotheses are imposed. First, $Q$ is stable under change of test ring: for all rings $A, A'$, every ring homomorphism $\varphi : A \to A'$ and every $u : \operatorname{Spec} A \to H$, if $Q\,A\,u$ holds then so does $Q\,A'$ applied to $\operatorname{Spec}\varphi$ followed by $u$. Second, $Q$ is Zariski-local in the test ring: if $r : \iota \to A$ is a family whose image spans the unit ideal of $A$ and $Q$ holds for the composite of $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the localisation map $A \to A_{r_i}$ with $u$ for every $i$, then $Q\,A\,u$ holds. Third, on every affine chart $Q$ is cut out by a finitely generated ideal: for every open subscheme $U$ of $H$ with $U$ affine, every ring $B$ and every isomorphism $e : \operatorname{Spec} B \cong U$, there is a finitely generated ideal $J \subseteq B$ such that for all rings $A$ and all $\varphi : B \to A$, the point $\operatorname{Spec}\varphi$ followed by $e$ followed by the inclusion $U \hookrightarrow H$ satisfies $Q$ if and only if the ideal of $A$ generated by $\varphi(J)$ is zero. The conclusion asserts the existence of a scheme $C$ and a morphism $\iota : C \to H$ which is a closed immersion and is locally of finite presentation, such that for every ring $A$ and every $u : \operatorname{Spec} A \to H$, there exists $v : \operatorname{Spec} A \to C$ with $v$ followed by $\iota$ equal to $u$ if and only if $Q\,A\,u$ holds.
--
--   This is the representability criterion for a closed subfunctor of (the functor of affine points of) a scheme, in the refined form where the local defining ideals are finitely generated and the representing closed immersion is consequently locally of finite presentation. It is used to produce the Hilbert-scheme-type closed subschemes of the project, namely the closed locus where a homogeneous ideal condition holds and the locus in a parameter scheme where a level structure is preserved by an isogeny pair.
